N2  - Diabetes is the leading cause of ESRD worldwide.1 With the global prevalence of diabetes increasing, rates of diabetes-induced ESRD are expected to rise, causing enormous strain on global economies as they treat this costly disease and creating a need for low-cost interventions to prevent progression of disease.1 It is known that lesions of diabetic nephropathy can be reversed with pancreas transplantation, but only after 5 years of normal glycemic levels.2 Since nearly 90% of type 2 diabetes is caused by lifestyle, we hypothesize that a similar reversal in diabetic nephropathy can be achieved with successful dietary interventions over time.3 We searched the scientific literature for lifestyle interventions that were successful in reversing diabetes. Given the ability of plant-based diets to reverse other chronic diseases, like heart disease, we limited our search to plant-based diets.4 We limit our discussion to several key articles. A recent meta-analysis of plant-based diets being used to treat diabetes showed that they were associated with an average reduction in HbA1c of 0.39% over conventional diets.5 Excluded from the meta-analysis was a randomized-controlled trial that showed a HbA1c decrease of 1.23% in patients adhering to a plant-based diet compared to a 0.38% decrease in a control group following an American Diabetes Association diet.6 The most impressive results, however, were seen in an retrospective case series of 13 individual who followed a primarily plant-based diet for 7 months. In that study, mean HbA1c fell from 8.2% to 5.8% with 8 patients having reversal of diabetes.7 Plant-based diets are capable of improving-and even reversing-diabetes in select patients. Based on results after pancreas transplantation, it is likely possible to reverse diabetic kidney lesions through less invasive means if normal glycemic levels can be achieved and maintained through dietary changes. This abstract is the first to hypothesize that reversing diabetic kidney lesions may be attainable via dietary changes. References available online.
