N2  - Vegetarian diet includes relatively large amounts of cereals, legumes, nuts, fruits, green leafy vegetables usually high in carbohydrates, n-6 fatty acids, beta carotene, folic acid, Vitamin C, Vitamin E, iron and magnesium whereas it is relatively low in protein, saturated fat, long chain n-3 fatty acids, retinol, vitamin B12, Vitamin D, calcium and zinc. There is inadequacy of micronutrients amongst vegetarians and suggesting a need to ensure daily intake of specific vitamins and minerals. Vegans have lower BMI and total serum cholesterol and reported to have reduced risk of cardio vascular disease, type 2 diabetes and certain forms of cancer. Whether these observations relate to dietary habits only or by a healthier lifestyle, less smoking, good physical activity is unsettled. The dairy food contains most of the micronutrients and minerals in sufficient quantity that can maintain the adequacy and good health if consumed regularly. More than two daily servings of dairy products significantly improves vitamin B12 status, lowers blood pressure and reduces adipose tissue mass. Hence, vegetarians if include dairy products in their daily diet (lactovegetarians) can assure that adequate amount of essential nutrients are consumed. The present review will highlight the role dairy products in vegetarians.
