N2  - A plant-based diet - a diet that encourages whole, plant-based foods and discourages meats, dairy products, and eggs as well as all refined and processed foods - aids in the management and prevention of multiple chronic diseases, including type 2 diabetes. However, minimal research has assessed patients' knowledge and perceptions of plant-based diets. In this ongoing qualitative study, we explored knowledge and barriers to eating plant-meals among adults with type 2 diabetes. To date, 11 adults with type 2 diabetes (age=53.7±10.2 years, A1c=7.1±0.7%, diabetes duration= 8.6±6.1 years, BMI=32.8±3.7, 45% female, 90% white) participated in interviews. Interviews were transcribed, coded, and analyzed via content and thematic analyses using NVivo 10 software. The majority of participants lacked knowledge about plant-based diets. Despite this lack of knowledge, most thought a plant-based diet was healthy due to increased fruit and vegetable consumption and fiber content. When asked about incorporating plant-based meals, all said they would be willing to consider adding a few during the week to their current diet. However, participants discussed multiple barriers, including lack of time, spoilage of produce, limited access to fresh fruits and vegetables, higher costs, not wanting to give up meat, and lack of support from family members and friends. Further, more than half of the participants expressed concern about increased fruit consumption and its perceived negative effect on blood glucose and A1c levels. Participants also voiced concerns regarding sufficient protein intake and the satiation level of plant-based meals. Type 2 diabetes patients may benefit from a highly structured, plant-based educational intervention that addresses what a plant-based diet is, why plant-based meals are a good choice for type 2 diabetes, and how to manage potential barriers, such as how to prevent produce spoilage and preparing a week's worth of frozen meals in one day to save time and money.
