N2  - Objectives: Diabetic peripheral neuropathy occurs in up to 60 percent of individuals with type 2 diabetes and is associated with significant morbidity, including gait disturbances, amputations, anxiety, depression and reduced quality of life. Available treatments are limited. Recent data suggest that plant-based dietary interventions typically improve glycemic control and other factors associated with type 2 diabetes and its complications. We hypothesized that such a diet would reduce painful symptoms of diabetic neuropathy. The current study was intended to investigate the efficacy of this approach and the suitability of the overall method, permitting larger trials to follow. Methods: Individuals having normal blood vitamin B12 levels with type 2 diabetes and painful diabetic neuropathy were randomly assigned to two groups. The intervention group was asked to follow a low-fat, plant-based diet, with weekly classes for support in following the prescribed diet, and to take a daily vitamin B12 supplement. The control group was asked to take the same vitamin B12 supplement but received no other intervention. At baseline, 20 weeks, and 1 year, biometric, biochemical and physical data were collected. Questionnaires included 2-day dietary records, an analogue 'worst pain' scale, the Michigan Neuropathy Screening Instrument, the global impression scale, the Short Form McGill Pain Questionnaire, the Neuropathy Total Symptom Score, a weekly pain diary and the Norfolk Quality of Life Questionnaire. Results: Thirty-five patients were enrolled into the study, with a mean age of 57 years. After 20 weeks, body weight change with the intervention was -6.4 kg (95% CI -9.4 to -3.4, p<0.001) in an effect size analysis. HbA1c for the intervention group decreased by 0.8 percentage points over 20 weeks but remained unchanged in the control group (p=0.07). Glucose-lowering medications were reduced for 10 intervention-group participants and one control-group participant. Electrochemical skin conductance in the foot improved by an average of 12.4 microSiemens (95% CI 1.2 to 23.6, p=0.03) with the intervention in an effect size analysis. The between-group difference in the change in pain, as measured by the McGill pain questionnaire, was -8.2 points (95% CI -16.1 to -0.3, p=0.04). The change in the Michigan Neuropathy Screening Instrument patient questionnaire score was -1.6 points (95% CI -3.0 to -0.2, p=0.03). The 1-year findings were similarly promising. Conclusion: This is the first randomized controlled study of diet and diabetic nerve pain. It suggests the potential of a dietary approach for treating diabetic neuropathy and provides findings that can be used to guide further studies.
