N2  - It has been reported that macronutrient oxidation and resting energy expenditure (REE) might be modified in response to changes in diet composition and that mitochondrial oxidation was significantly activated after low glycemic index diet. The aim of our study was to compare the effects of vegetarian (VD) and conventional diabetic diet (CD) with the same caloric restriction (-500 kcal/ day) on REE after a 12-weeks-diet-intervention and additional 12-weeks of diet combined with aerobic exercise in subjects with type 2 diabetes (T2D). Open, parallel randomized study. 74 subjects with T2D were randomly assigned to either VD or CD with the same caloric restriction (-500 kcal/day). Indirect calorimetry was performed using metabolic monitor VMAX; Sensor Medics, Anaheim, CA, USA at start, at weeks 12 (after diet intervention) and 24 (after additional 12 weeks of diet + aerobic exercise). Predicted REE was counted according to Harris Benedict equation. ANOVA for repeated measures was used for statistical analyses. REE increased in both groups after 12 weeks (p<0.001). After week 24 it remained constant in VD whereas it decreased in CD (p=0.03). There was a trend toward greater increase in REE from baseline to 24 weeks in VD (by 14% vs. 8% in CD; group x time p=0.07). If counted as percentage of the predicted REE according to Harris Benedict equation, it increased in both groups; the increase was greater in VD (by 18% vs. 9% in CD; group x time p=0.04). Respiratory quotient decreased in both groups from baseline both to 12 weeks (p<0.01) and to 24 weeks (p<0.001) as implicated by increase in fasting oxidation (FO) of fat in both groups (p<0.001). FO of carbohydrates decreased in CD by 89% (p=0.01) while it did not change in VD. FO of protein did not change in either group. We have observed greater increase in REE in VD. Lower glycemic index of VD, higher FO of carbohydrates and possible increase in mitochondrial oxidation may be partly responsible for greater increase in REE with VD after aerobic exercise.
