N2  - Results: The aim of our study was to evaluate the effect of vegetarian diet (VD) compared to conventional diabetic diet (CD) with similar caloric restriction on insulin secretion (IS) and physical fitness (PF) in subjects with type 2 diabetes (T2D) after a 3-months- intervention of diet and aerobic exercise program. Open, parallel randomized study. 70 subjects with T2D were randomly assigned to either experimental group (EG) following VD or control group (CG) following CD with similar caloric restriction (-500 kcal/day). All meals during the study were provided. Participants were prescribed an individualized exercise program. IS was evaluated after standard meal stimulation; PF was measured by spiroergometry at start and after 12 weeks. Decrease of stimulated glucose plasma concentrations was significant only in EG, but the difference between groups was not significant (interaction between group and time p=0.4). Decrease of stimulated immunoreactive insulin plasma concentrations was significant only in EG (interaction between group and time p<0.001). Decrease of stimulated C-peptide plasma concentrations was significant only in EG (interaction between group and time p<0.01). Systolic blood pressure at maximal performance (MP) did not change in either group. Diastolic blood pressure at MP decreased in both groups (p<0.001): by 8% in EG, and by 6% in CG, with no difference between groups. Heart rate at MP did not change in either group. Maximal oxygen consumption (VO2 max) increased by 12% in EG, whereas it did not change in CG (between-group p<0.01). MP (Watt max) increased by 21% in EG, whereas it did not change in CG (between-group p<0.05). Our results indicate that VD leads more effectively to improvement in IS and PF than CD after aerobic exercise program. VD could be a more convenient alternative in treatment of T2D.
