N2  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: Identify metformin as a potential cause of vitamin B12 deficiency. LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: Discuss screening for vitamin B12 deficiency in patients taking metformin. CASE: A 57-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, hypothyroidism, and morbid obesity was seen in clinic for follow-up after a recent hospitalization for a respiratory illness. She had been started on metformin 500 mg BID in March 2009 for her diabetes, and the medication had been effective in lowering her HgbA1C and promoting weight loss. Routine labs drawn during hospitalization showed an MCV of 102 fL and a hematocrit of 34.5%. A diagnosis of megaloblastic anemia was entertained, and the patient was worked-up for vitamin B12 deficiency. Although her B12 level was low-normal at 252 pg/mL (normal 240-900), both her methylmalomic acid and homocysteine levels were elevated at 343 nmol/L (normal 87-318) and 80.7 umol/L (normal 4.5-12.4) respectively. After receiving this new diagnosis, the patient asked about metformin contributing to her B12 deficiency. The package insert for metformin suggests that “serum B12 levels be monitored every 2-3 years in patients predisposed to low vitamin B12.” However, although metformin-induced B12 deficiency was considered, her labs returned positive for antibodies to intrinsic factor, consistent with pernicious anemia, and she was treated with parenteral B12 injections. DISCUSSION: Vitamin B12 deficiency, as defined by low levels of cobalamin or high levels of methylmalonic acid and homocysteine, is a common cause of megaloblastic anemia. Numerous etiologies have been identified for the condition, including pernicious anemia, gastrointestinal abnormalities (as in Crohn's Disease), and a strict vegan diet. However, other factors may contribute to low levels of cobalamin, including certain drugs like metformin, which block absorption of the vitamin. Various studies have demonstrated decreased levels of vitamin B12 in patients taking this oral biguanide. A randomized,placebocontrolled trial in the Journal of Internal Medicine concluded that in patients with type 2 diabetes, 16 weeks of treatment with metformin was associated with reduced levels of folate and vitamin B12 and moderate increases of homocysteine.[1] A 2006 case-control study in Archives of Internal Medicine demonstrated similar findings, related to metformin dose and treatment duration.[2] The mechanism of this effect is not well understood, but likely results from malabsorption of vitamin B12 and intrinsic factor (IF) in the ileum. Uptake of the B12/IF complex is a calcium-mediated process, and metformin interferes with intestinal calcium channels; as such, calcium supplementation may be effective in reversing this condition.[3] Metformin remains a valuable tool in lowering HgbA1C in patients with type 2 diabetes; however, providers must be aware of the association between its use and vitamin B12 deficiency, especially in patients on long-term therapy and/or taking high doses. Patients should be queried about neurologic problems and screened for signs and symptoms of anemia. Adherence to a diet rich in folic acid/ B12 should be encouraged, and calcium supplementation may be a reasonable option as well. Serum B12 should be monitored at least every 2-3 years, or more frequently in the face of alarming symptoms or laboratory abnormalities. [1] Wulffele, M. G et. al Effects of short term treatment with metformin on serum concentrations of homocysteine, folate, and vitamin B12 in type 2 diabetes mellitus: a randominzed, placebo-controlled trial.
